<h2>Forty-eight years in four paragraphs</h2>
<p>The appellant joined the Bank in 1969 and was suspended in 1978 amidst criminal allegations of which he was acquitted in 1983. A departmental charge-sheet issued in 1990 was quashed by the Punjab &amp; Haryana High Court in 2007, with reinstatement and consequential benefits; the Bank&rsquo;s intra-court appeal and SLP both failed. He retired in 2009; the arrears — about Rs 42 lakh — were substantially paid only in December 2011, after contempt proceedings. The fight then shifted to interest on that delayed money: a fresh writ of 2015 was dismissed in 2023, and a miscellaneous application filed in the original 1991 writ was rejected by the single judge and the Division Bench. The Supreme Court heard the matter against that last rejection.</p>

<h2>The procedural holding</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>What he filed</strong><p>A miscellaneous application in a writ petition allowed back in 2007 — asking, in substance, for a new relief: interest on delayed arrears.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>What the law required</strong><p>A fresh proceeding for the subsequent cause of action — which he had in fact filed in 2015 — and an intra-court appeal when that writ was wrongly dismissed.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The Court&rsquo;s treatment of the maintainability question is a compact restatement. A miscellaneous application in a decided case is &ldquo;procedural, not substantive&rdquo;: it can seek clarification, correction of accidental errors, or recall in the narrow circumstances the law recognises — the judgment surveys Brahma Dutt Sharma, Budhia Swain, Ram Chandra Singh, K.A. Ansari and K.L. Rathi Steels — but it cannot reopen merits, add reliefs, or litigate events that post-date the decision.</p>

<h2>The substantive holding: subsequent causes of action survive</h2>
<p>Equally significant is what the Court refused to hold. The Bank argued constructive res judicata: interest should have been claimed earlier. The Court disagreed — res judicata and Order II Rule 2 apply only to claims that could have been raised when the earlier petition was instituted, and a right to relief ordinarily relates back to the date proceedings are instituted. Interest for delay that accumulated during the litigation was by definition unavailable in 1991. The claim was therefore never barred; it was merely pursued, at the final step, through the wrong vehicle. The distinction matters far beyond service law: in writ, civil and commercial practice alike, events arising after judgment generate fresh causes of action that deserve fresh proceedings — not miscellaneous applications, and not the res judicata axe either.</p>

<h2>Article 142 as the closing instrument</h2>
<p>Having found the procedural route misconceived but the underlying equity real — no court had ever denied the appellant&rsquo;s entitlement to interest, and the money he eventually received had been withheld for years — the Court declined to condemn a septuagenarian to another round of litigation. It directed the Bank to pay an additional Rs 5,00,000 within two months as full and final settlement. The judgment thus pairs doctrinal strictness with practical mercy: the taxonomy of miscellaneous applications is preserved intact, and the litigant is not made to pay for his counsel&rsquo;s choice of door.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>After final judgment, ask first: is the grievance ancillary (clarification, correction, compliance) or substantive (new relief, new events)? Only the former belongs in a miscellaneous application.</p>
  <p>Diarise subsequent causes of action — delay interest, post-decree developments — for fresh proceedings with their own limitation discipline.</p>
  <p>When a fresh proceeding is wrongly dismissed, appeal it; collateral attempts in the old file waste the very equity that supports the claim.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information only and is not legal advice.</div>
